theory.intro:
Two identical trapeziums fit together to make a parallelogram with base $a+b$ and height $h$. One trapezium is half of that parallelogram, so its area is half of $h(a+b)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (H) at (1,0);
\coordinate (D) at (1,2);
\coordinate (E) at (4,0);
\draw (0,0) -- (4,0) node[midway,below] {$b$};
\draw (4,0) -- (6,0) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (6,0) -- (7,2);
\draw (7,2) -- (3,2) node[midway,above] {$b$};
\draw (3,2) -- (1,2) node[midway,above] {$a$};
\draw (1,2) -- (0,0);
\draw[dashed] (4,0) -- (3,2);
\draw[dashed] (D) -- (H) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(H)!0.25cm!(D)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(H)!0.25cm!(E)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(H)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
The parallel sides may be drawn top and bottom, left and right, or on a slant; the formula is unchanged.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (0,3.2);
\coordinate (R) at (3,2.6);
\coordinate (S) at (3,0.8);
\coordinate (F) at (0,1.4);
\coordinate (G) at (3,1.4);
\draw (P) -- (Q) node[pos=0.35,left] {$b$};
\draw (Q) -- (R);
\draw (R) -- (S) node[midway,right] {$a$};
\draw (S) -- (P);
\draw[->] (0,2.4) -- (0,2.9);
\draw[->] (3,1.9) -- (3,2.3);
\draw[dashed] (F) -- (G) node[midway,above] {$h$};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(F)!0.25cm!(G)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(F)!0.25cm!(Q)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(F)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

